WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.112
Autonomy is a dial, not a switch.

2
00:00:02.392 --> 00:00:05.997
Hugging Face’s smolagents documentation (Apache 2.

3
00:00:06.277 --> 00:00:15.856
0, adapted here) offers a sharper way to see the line: agency is a spectrum, measured by how much the model’s output controls your program’s flow.

4
00:00:16.136 --> 00:00:20.744
The table below adapts its levels and adds where each fits in Anthropic’s terms.

5
00:00:21.024 --> 00:00:24.288
Read it from top to bottom as handing over more control.

6
00:00:24.568 --> 00:00:28.195
At the top, model output is just data your code processes.

7
00:00:28.475 --> 00:00:30.821
Next, it flips a switch your code defined.

8
00:00:31.101 --> 00:00:34.365
Then it chooses which function runs and with what arguments.

9
00:00:34.645 --> 00:00:37.205
Then it decides whether the loop continues at all.

10
00:00:37.485 --> 00:00:39.768
Finally, one agent can start others.

11
00:00:40.048 --> 00:00:42.949
Two consequences matter for you as the engineer.

12
00:00:43.229 --> 00:00:55.432
First, each step down multiplies the number of possible executions, which is what makes agents capable of novel paths and also what makes them hard to test: you cannot enumerate the trajectories.

13
00:00:55.712 --> 00:00:57.952
Second, you can usually choose the level.

14
00:00:58.232 --> 00:01:06.232
Most product features are best served at the top two or three rows, with a model making bounded choices inside code you can read.

15
00:01:06.512 --> 00:01:19.163
The smolagents guide puts it plainly: if a predetermined workflow fits your queries, code it, because it is more reliable; reach for agentic behavior when the predetermined workflow falls short too often.
